"
    localparam QUEUE_READ_LATENCY = queue_carriage_READ_LATENCY(
        ASYNC,
        CONFLICT_PROOF,
        REGISTERED_IN,
        REGISTERED_IN_BRAM,
        REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM
    )
"
- defined as
"
    function automatic int queue_carriage_READ_LATENCY(
        int ASYNC,
        int CONFLICT_PROOF,
        int REGISTERED_IN,
        int REGISTERED_IN_BRAM,
        int REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM
    );
        if(ASYNC == 1) return queue_async_READ_LATENCY(REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM);
        return queue_READ_LATENCY(CONFLICT_PROOF, REGISTERED_IN, REGISTERED_IN_BRAM, REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM);
    endfunction
"
- all arguments required by the selected queue latency function are passed correctly
- when ASYNC is 1, queue_async_READ_LATENCY() only requires REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM because
  the asynchronous queue does not expose REGISTERED_IN or REGISTERED_IN_BRAM options
- when ASYNC is 0, queue_READ_LATENCY() receives the synchronous queue's complete read
  pipeline configuration
- both latency functions return at least one cycle, so the later packed range
  '[QUEUE_READ_LATENCY - 1 : 0]' always has a valid positive width
- this localparam must remain consistent with the selected queue instantiation because it
  aligns queue_pop with queue_rd_data before the data enters queue_pop_coupler
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
    // Write side
    input wr_clk_i,
    input wr_rst_i,

    input  [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] wr_data_i,
    input                       wr_valid_i,
    output                      wr_ready_o,
    output                      wr_burstready_o,

    // Read side
    input rd_clk_i,
    input rd_rst_i,

    output [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] rd_data_o,
    output                      rd_valid_o,
    output                      rd_burstvalid_o,
    input                       rd_ready_i
"
- the write side matches the upstream-facing interface of queue_push_coupler
    - wr_data_i and wr_valid_i enter the carriage
    - wr_ready_o and wr_burstready_o return flow-control information upstream
- the read side matches the downstream-facing interface of queue_pop_coupler
    - rd_data_o, rd_valid_o and rd_burstvalid_o leave the carriage
    - rd_ready_i supplies downstream flow control
- both datapaths are exactly DATA_WIDTH bits wide
- separate write and read clocks/resets are required for queue_async
- when ASYNC is 0, wr_clk_i and rd_clk_i must be the same clock and both reset inputs
  must represent the same reset; the synchronous queue itself is clocked by wr_clk_i
- reset must be held for several cycles and followed by idle cycles before traffic starts;
  this is especially important because queue_async registers its internal reset release
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] queue_wr_data;
    logic                      queue_push;
    logic                      queue_full;
    logic                      queue_write_lookahead;
    logic [ADDR_WIDTH : 0]     queue_more_than_or_eq;

    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0] queue_rd_data;
    logic                      queue_rd_valid;
    logic                      queue_pop;
    logic                      queue_empty;
    logic                      queue_read_lookahead;
    logic [ADDR_WIDTH : 0]     queue_less_than_or_eq;
"
- queue_wr_data and queue_push carry write transactions from queue_push_coupler into the
  selected queue
- queue_full and queue_write_lookahead carry write-side occupancy information back to
  queue_push_coupler
- queue_more_than_or_eq is ADDR_WIDTH + 1 bits because it represents an occupancy value
  from zero through the complete queue depth, not a queue address
- queue_rd_data and queue_pop connect the selected queue to queue_pop_coupler
- queue_rd_valid is generated locally because neither queue nor queue_async exposes a
  read-valid output
- queue_empty and queue_read_lookahead carry read-side occupancy information into
  queue_pop_coupler
- queue_less_than_or_eq is also intentionally ADDR_WIDTH + 1 bits because it is an
  occupancy threshold
- the threshold signals are consumed by queue in synchronous mode; in asynchronous mode,
  queue_async supplies fixed one-entry lookahead outputs instead
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
    queue_push_coupler #(
        .DATA_WIDTH(DATA_WIDTH),
        .SIMPLE(PUSH_SIMPLE),
        .ASYNC(ASYNC),
        .BURST_SIZE(PUSH_BURST_SIZE),
        .ADDR_WIDTH(ADDR_WIDTH)
    ) push_coupler (
        .wr_clk_i(wr_clk_i),
        .wr_rst_i(wr_rst_i),

        .wr_data_i(wr_data_i),
        .wr_valid_i(wr_valid_i),
        .ready_o(wr_ready_o),
        .burstready_o(wr_burstready_o),

        .full_i(queue_full),
        .lookahead_i(queue_write_lookahead),

        .wr_data_o(queue_wr_data),
        .push_o(queue_push),
        .more_than_or_eq_o(queue_more_than_or_eq)
    );
"
- all queue_push_coupler parameters are mapped to the corresponding carriage parameters
- the upstream carriage write interface connects directly to the coupler fill side
- the selected queue's full and lookahead outputs control the coupler drain side
- queue_wr_data and queue_push are the only write data/control signals presented to the
  selected queue
- queue_more_than_or_eq supplies the programmable occupancy threshold required by queue
  in synchronous mode
- in asynchronous mode, queue_write_lookahead instead comes directly from
  queue_async.one_space_left_o; queue_more_than_or_eq is then intentionally unused
- there is no additional full/reset gating on queue_push, preserving the coupler's purpose
  of avoiding added combinational logic in the queue control path
- when ASYNC and PUSH_SIMPLE are both 1, PUSH_BURST_SIZE must be 1 for a usable
  wr_burstready_o because queue_async only supplies one-entry write lookahead
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
        if(ASYNC == 1) begin : queue_async_g
            queue_async #(
                .ADDR_WIDTH(ADDR_WIDTH),
                .DATA_WIDTH(DATA_WIDTH),
                .REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM(REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM),
                .SYNC_STAGES(SYNC_STAGES)
            ) queue_inst (
                .wr_clk_i(wr_clk_i),
                .wr_rst_i(wr_rst_i),

                .push_i(queue_push),
                .wr_data_i(queue_wr_data),
                .full_o(queue_full),
                .one_space_left_o(queue_write_lookahead),

                .rd_clk_i(rd_clk_i),
                .rd_rst_i(rd_rst_i),

                .pop_i(queue_pop),
                .rd_data_o(queue_rd_data),
                .empty_o(queue_empty),
                .one_item_left_o(queue_read_lookahead)
            );
"
- this branch is elaborated only when ASYNC is 1
- ADDR_WIDTH, DATA_WIDTH, REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM and SYNC_STAGES are passed to queue_async
  without modification
- the write and read sides use their respective clocks and resets
- queue_push, queue_wr_data, queue_full and queue_write_lookahead form the complete
  queue_async write-side connection
- queue_pop, queue_rd_data, queue_empty and queue_read_lookahead form the complete
  queue_async read-side connection
- one_space_left_o and one_item_left_o are the exact fixed lookahead signals expected by
  the push and pop couplers in asynchronous mode
- queue_async CDC bus-coherence constraints remain required outside the RTL
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
        end else begin : queue_sync_g
            queue #(
                .ADDR_WIDTH(ADDR_WIDTH),
                .DATA_WIDTH(DATA_WIDTH),
                .CONFLICT_PROOF(CONFLICT_PROOF),
                .REGISTERED_IN(REGISTERED_IN),
                .REGISTERED_IN_BRAM(REGISTERED_IN_BRAM),
                .REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM(REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM)
            ) queue_inst (
                .clk_i(wr_clk_i),
                .rst_i(wr_rst_i),

                .push_i(queue_push),
                .wr_data_i(queue_wr_data),

                .pop_i(queue_pop),
                .rd_data_o(queue_rd_data),

                .full_o(queue_full),
                .empty_o(queue_empty),

                .less_than_i(queue_less_than_or_eq),
                .less_than_o(queue_read_lookahead),

                .more_than_i(queue_more_than_or_eq),
                .more_than_o(queue_write_lookahead)
            );
"
- this branch is elaborated only when ASYNC is 0
- all synchronous queue parameters are passed without modification
- queue is clocked and reset from wr_clk_i/wr_rst_i; therefore rd_clk_i/rd_rst_i must be
  driven identically in synchronous mode so queue_pop_coupler remains in the same domain
- queue_push and queue_wr_data connect the push coupler to the queue write port
- queue_pop and queue_rd_data connect the queue read port to the pop coupler
- queue_more_than_or_eq drives queue.more_than_i, and queue.more_than_o returns the
  write-side lookahead result expected by queue_push_coupler
- queue_less_than_or_eq drives queue.less_than_i, and queue.less_than_o returns the
  read-side lookahead result expected by queue_pop_coupler
- queue's threshold comparisons are inclusive despite their legacy less_than/more_than
  names, matching the couplers' less_than_or_eq/more_than_or_eq meanings
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
    logic [QUEUE_READ_LATENCY - 1 : 0] queue_rd_valid_pipeline;
    always@(posedge rd_clk_i) begin
        if(rd_rst_i) begin
            queue_rd_valid_pipeline <= '0;
        end else begin
            queue_rd_valid_pipeline[0] <= queue_pop;
            for(int stage = 1; stage < QUEUE_READ_LATENCY; stage++) begin
                queue_rd_valid_pipeline[stage] <= queue_rd_valid_pipeline[stage - 1];
            end
        end
    end

    assign queue_rd_valid = queue_rd_valid_pipeline[QUEUE_READ_LATENCY - 1];
"
- queue_pop represents each read request accepted by the selected queue because
  queue_pop_coupler only asserts it while its empty/lookahead control permits a pop
- queue_pop is intentionally not combined with queue_empty here; adding queue_empty would
  create an unnecessary combinational path into the read-valid pipeline and undermine the
  reservoir-based timing isolation
- the valid bit is delayed by exactly QUEUE_READ_LATENCY read-clock cycles, aligning it
  with queue_rd_data for both queue and queue_async
- stage zero captures queue_pop, and every later stage shifts the captured validity by one
  additional cycle
- nonblocking assignments preserve the old value of each preceding stage during a shift
- rd_rst_i clears every pipeline stage, preventing pre-reset pop history from being marked
  valid after reset
- the required post-reset idle cycles allow the selected queue and this validity pipeline
  to settle before new traffic begins
- queue_rd_valid selects the final pipeline stage, which is aligned with queue_rd_data and
  is supplied to queue_pop_coupler as its fill-valid signal
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

"
    queue_pop_coupler #(
        .DATA_WIDTH(DATA_WIDTH),
        .ADDR_WIDTH(ADDR_WIDTH),
        .ASYNC(ASYNC),
        .BURSTMARK(POP_BURSTMARK),
        .CONFLICT_PROOF(CONFLICT_PROOF),
        .REGISTERED_IN(REGISTERED_IN),
        .REGISTERED_IN_BRAM(REGISTERED_IN_BRAM),
        .REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM(REGISTERED_OUT_BRAM)
    ) pop_coupler (
        .rd_clk_i(rd_clk_i),
        .rd_rst_i(rd_rst_i),

        .empty_i(queue_empty),
        .lookahead_i(queue_read_lookahead),
        .rd_data_i(queue_rd_data),
        .rd_valid_i(queue_rd_valid),

        .pop_o(queue_pop),
        .less_than_or_eq_o(queue_less_than_or_eq),

        .rd_data_o(rd_data_o),
        .rd_valid_o(rd_valid_o),
        .burstvalid_o(rd_burstvalid_o),

        .ready_i(rd_ready_i)
    );
"
- all queue_pop_coupler parameters are mapped to the corresponding carriage parameters
- ASYNC and the queue registration parameters also determine the coupler's calculated
  spool-up and reservoir depths, so they must match the selected queue configuration
- queue_empty, queue_read_lookahead, queue_rd_data and the locally aligned queue_rd_valid
  form the complete queue-side fill interface
- queue_pop requests data from the selected queue
- queue_less_than_or_eq supplies the synchronous queue occupancy threshold; it is
  intentionally unused by queue_async, whose one_item_left_o supplies lookahead directly
- rd_data_o, rd_valid_o and rd_burstvalid_o expose the pop reservoir drain interface
- rd_ready_i controls downstream consumption and may be driven from a downstream
  burst-ready condition when complete bursts are required
- the pop reservoir absorbs the selected queue's read latency and stop latency, allowing
  queue_pop to remain a registered control signal without adding queue_empty gating here
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// AI verification summary
- every external output is driven in both ASYNC generate configurations
- only one queue implementation is elaborated for a given ASYNC value
- write-side and read-side data widths remain DATA_WIDTH throughout the carriage
- occupancy values use [ADDR_WIDTH : 0], while queue memory addresses remain internal to
  the selected queue at [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]
- synchronous mode requires common clocks/resets; asynchronous mode requires CDC constraints
- reset and post-reset idle requirements are part of the module integration contract
- parameter legality is inherited from the instantiated modules and is not guarded locally
[Marc103 - 08/11/26]
